  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏰 Medieval Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Real medieval English names, with surnames from jobs, places, fathers and nicknames."
    },
    "intro": "Most English surnames began in the Middle Ages, as a way to tell all the Johns and Williams apart. They came from four places: a job (Fletcher, the arrow-maker), a place (atte Wode, at the wood), a father (FitzWalter, Walter's son) or a nickname (Armstrong). This **medieval name generator** uses real first names from the period and real surnames of each kind, with what they meant.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Four kinds of medieval surname",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Occupational:</strong> Smith, Baker, Fletcher (arrow-maker), Cooper (barrel-maker), Chandler (candle-maker), Webster (weaver), and Chaucer, from the French for a maker of shoes or hose.</li><li><strong>Locative:</strong> where someone lived, often written with <em>atte</em>, \"at the\": atte Wode became Atwood, atte Welle became Atwell.</li><li><strong>Patronymic:</strong> a father's name. Norman families used <em>Fitz</em>, from the French for son (FitzWalter); others added -son (Johnson, Watson from Wat, a short form of Walter).</li><li><strong>Nicknames:</strong> Brown, Long, Armstrong (strong in the arm), Russell (red-haired).</li></ul><p>Surnames only settled into family names gradually, between about the twelfth and fourteenth centuries; before that a byname could change with a new job or a new home.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The first names",
        "content": "<p>The first names here are ones that were common in England after the Norman Conquest: John, William, Robert, Richard and Walter for men; Alice, Agnes, Matilda (or Maud), Joan and Isabel for women. A few, like Walkelin, Rohese and Hawise, have fallen out of use and sound wonderfully strange today.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Give the surname a reason.</strong> A Fletcher who can't make arrows, or an atte Welle who lives nowhere near a well, has a story.</li><li><strong>Use nicknames freely.</strong> Medieval people did: Wat for Walter, Hob for Robert, Dick for Richard.</li><li><strong>For knights and nobles,</strong> try the <a href=\"knight-name-generator.html\">Knight Name Generator</a>; for the Anglo-Saxons before the Conquest, the <a href=\"anglo-saxon-name-generator.html\">Anglo-Saxon Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How did medieval people get surnames?",
        "answer": "From a job (Fletcher), a place (atte Wode), a father (FitzWalter, Johnson) or a nickname (Armstrong). They became fixed family names gradually, between about the 12th and 14th centuries."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does Fitz mean?",
        "answer": "Son of, from the Norman French fiz. FitzWalter means Walter's son."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these real medieval names?",
        "answer": "Yes. The first names were in common use in England after 1066, and the surnames are real medieval surnames with their original meanings."
